    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_86827bc4",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: john wilkes booth be like; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme depicts John Wilkes Booth, the assassin of Abraham Lincoln, walking up a staircase into the clouds, with text implying he is lying about Lincoln's death by claiming Lincoln isn't 'up here.' The overall intent is to mock Booth's actions and possibly his perceived delusions or lies. While not targeting any specific protected group, the meme's harmfulness stems from its potential to trivialize a grave historical event — the assassination of a U.S. president — and the resulting national trauma. It uses dark humor to suggest Booth is either delusional or dishonest, which can be seen as disrespectful to the gravity of the event and the victims, including Lincoln's family and the nation. The image's visual metaphor (ascending into clouds) is juxtaposed with the text to create a satirical take on Booth's supposed 'delusion' or 'cover-up,' which can be interpreted as a form of historical revisionism or disrespect for the solemnity of the assassination. The meme's intent is to provoke laughter at the expense of a historical tragedy, which can be considered harmful due to its potential to diminish the seriousness of the event and the suffering it caused.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_7a4d7008"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
